{
    name: "dma"
    import_testplans: ["hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/csr_testplan.hjson",
                       "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/alert_test_testplan.hjson",
                       "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/intr_test_testplan.hjson",
                       "hw/dv/tools/dvsim/testplans/tl_device_access_types_testplan.hjson"]
    testpoints: [
      //--------------------------------------------------------------------
      // Tests for DMA DUT 'memory-to-memory' and 'hardware handshake' modes
      //--------------------------------------------------------------------
      {
        name: dma_memory_smoke
        desc: '''
              Smoke test in which random DMA operations are performed with 'memory-to-memory' mode

              Stimulus:
                - Configure DMA
                  * Randomize DMA memory region base and limit
                  * Randomize source and destination address space IDs and address pointers such that all valid source and destination combinations are covered at least once
                  * Randomize Total transfer width and transaction size
                  * Update DMAC.op_code and disable hardware_handshake mode
                - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
                - Wait for TLUL transactions on the output interface
                - Respond with data from the TLUL agent in the testbench
                - Wait for the operation to complete
                - Repeat the operation randomly with different source and destination parameters

              Checking:
                - Check that the data presented at the source TLUL interface appears unchanged on the destination TLUL interface
                - Check for the assertion of PLIC interrupt if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable else check for DMAC.DONE after every iteration
                - Check for any spurious requests from the remaining TLUL interfaces
                - Check if the number of requests on TLUL agent matches the expected data size
                - Check if DMAC.busy bit is set throughout the operation in each iteration
              '''
        stage: V1
        tests: ["dma_memory_smoke"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_handshake_smoke
        desc: '''
              Smoke test in which 'hardware handshake' DMA operations are performed

              Stimulus:
                - Configure DMA
                  * Randomize DMA memory region base and limit
                  * Randomize source and destination address space IDs and address pointers such that all valid source and destination combinations are covered at least once
                  * Randomize total transfer width and transaction sizes
                  * Program DMAC.op_code and enable 'hardware handshake' mode
                - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Assert Low Speed IO (LSIO) interrupt
              - Wait for TLUL transaction on the output interface
              - Respond with random number of data items from TLUL agent in the testbench
              - Deassert LSIO interrupt
              - Wait for the operation to complete
              - Repeat the copy operation with different source and destination parameters

              Checking:
              - Check if DMA operation is completed successfully
                * Check that the data presented at the source TLUL interface appears unchanged on the destination TLUL interface
                * Check for assertion of PLIC interrupt if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable else check for DMAC.DONE after every iteration
                * Check if the number of requests on TLUL agent matches the expected data size
              - Check if STATUS.busy bit is set until 'hardware handshake' mode is disabled
              - Check if STATUS.done is set after LSIO interrupt is deasserted
              '''
        stage: V1
        tests: ["dma_handshake_smoke"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_generic_smoke
        desc: '''
              Smoke test with both 'memory-to-memory' and 'hardware handshaking' transfers.

              Stimulus:
              - FW allocates DMA enabled Memory Space for the data movement
              - FW configures Source Address and ASID
              - FW configures Destination Address and ASID
              - FW completes other configuration such as:
                  i)    Operation Size
                  ii)   Opcode
              - FW triggers the DMA operation
              - FW either
                  i)    Poll for completion
                  ii)   Waits for Completion Interrupt
              - Reset memory contents at the end of iteration
              '''
        stage: V1
        tests: ["dma_generic_smoke"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_memory_region_lock
        desc:'''
              Test to check DMA memory region lock functionality

              Stimulus:
                - Configure DMA
                  * Randomize DMA memory region base and limit
                  * Randomize source and destination address space IDs and address pointers such that all valid source and destination combinations are covered at least once
                  * Randomize Total transfer width and transaction size
                  * Lock DMA memory configuration registers
                  * Program DMAC register with OP code and randomize 'hardware handshake' mode enable bit
                - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Randomly change the DMA memory region base and limit
              - Wait for the operation to complete
              - Reset the design by asserting rst_n
              - Repeat DMA operation without locking the DMA memory configuration registers

              Checking:
              - Check that DMA operation is completed successfully
                * Check for assertion of interrupt if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable else check for DMAC.DONE after every iteration
              - Check if writes to DMA memory region and base values are ignored until range_regwen.regwen is cleared
              - Check if the range_regwen.regwen is set to MuBi4True after deassertion of reset
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_memory_region_lock"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_memory_tl_error
        desc: '''
              Test to check TLUL error behavior on DMA interfaces

              Stimulus:
              - Configure DMA to perform a copy operation with different source and destination parameters in 'memory-to-memory' mode
              - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Respond with an error on either source or destination TLUL interface request
              - Wait for PLIC interrupt assertion if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable is set else poll for DMAC.ABORTED bit

              Checking:
              - Check if STATUS.error bit is set (This indicates DMA operation is aborted)
              - Check that there are no TLUL transactions on source and destination interfaces after error response
              - Check if DMA generates an alert
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_memory_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_handshake_tl_error
        desc: '''
              Test to check TLUL error behavior on DMA interfaces with 'hardware handshake' mode

              Stimulus:
              - Configure DMA to perform a copy operation with different source and destination parameters in hardware_handshake mode
              - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Respond with an error on either source or destination TLUL interface request
              - Wait for PLIC interrupt assertion if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable is set else poll for DMAC.DONE bit

              Checking:
              - Check if STATUS.error bit is set (This indicates DMA operation is aborted)
              - Check that there are no TLUL transactions on source and destination interfaces after error response
              - Check if DMA generates an alert
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_handshake_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_handshake_stress
        desc: '''
              Stress test in which hardware handshake DMA operations are performed. Goal is to achieve maximum possible coverage of DMA configuration space in 'hardware handshake' mode

              Stimulus:
                - Configure DMA
                  * Randomize DMA memory region base and limit
                  * Randomize source and destination address space IDs and address pointers
                    such that all valid source and destination combinations are covered at least once
                  * Randomize total transfer width and transaction size
                  * Randomize DMAC.data_direction
                  * Randomize DMAC.FIFO_addr_auto_increment_enable and DMAC.memory_addr_auto_increment_enable
                  * Program DMAC register with OP code and enable 'hardware handshake' mode
                - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Assert Low Speed IO (LSIO) interrupt
              - Wait for TLUL transaction on the output interface
              - Respond with random number of data items from TLUL agent in the testbench
              - Deassert LSIO interrupt
              - Wait for the operation to complete

              Checking:
              - Check if DMA operation is completed successfully in each iteration
                * Check if DMAC.GO bit is not set until the DMA operation is complete
                * Check if STATUS.busy bit is set until 'hardware handshake' mode is disabled
                * Check if STATUS.done is set after LSIO interrupt is deasserted
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_handshake_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_memory_stress
        desc:'''
             Stress test in which random DMA operations are performed with 'memory-to-memory' mode. Goal is to achieve maximum possible coverage of DMA configuration space in 'memory-to-memory' mode

             Stimulus:
              - Configure DMA
                * Randomize DMA memory region base and limit
                * Randomize source and destination address space IDs and address pointers
                * Randomize Total transfer width and transaction size
                * Program DMAC register with OP code and disable 'hardware handshake' mode
              - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Wait for the operation to complete
              - Repeat operation with different source and destination addresses

             Checking:
              - Check that DMA operation is completed successfully
                * Check that the number of TLUL requests matches the expected data size
                * Check that the data presented at the source TLUL interface appears unchanged on the destination TLUL interface
                * Check if STATUS.busy bit is set until the copy operation is complete
                * Check if STATUS.done bit is set after the copy operation
             '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_memory_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_generic_stress
        desc: '''
              Stress test which combines `dma_memory_stress` and `dma_handshake_stress` to ensure that both 'memory-to-memory' and 'hardware handshaking' modes are exercised together without intervening resets.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_generic_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_handshake_mem_buffer_overflow
        desc: '''
              Test to check DMA memory buffer threshold and limit behavior with 'hardware handshake' mode

              Stimulus:
              - Configure DMA for 'hardware handshake' mode
              - Set memory_buffer_addr_auto_increment
              - Start DMA operation by setting DMAC.GO bit
              - Assert Low Speed IO (LSIO) interrupt
              - Wait for TLUL transaction on the output interface
              - Deassert LSIO interrupt
              - Wait for the operation to complete
              - Repeat the operation with different source and destination parameters

              Checking:
              - Check if DMA operation is completed successfully in each iteration
                * Check if DMAC.GO bit is set until the DMA operation is complete
                * Check if STATUS.busy bit is set until 'hardware handshake' mode is disabled
                * Check if STATUS.done is set after LSIO interrupt is deasserted
              - Check if PLIC interrupt is asserted when memory_buffer_auto_increment is enabled
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_handshake_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_abort
        desc: '''
              Test to check DMAC.abort functionality

              Stimulus:
              - Configure DMA to perform a copy operation randomly with different source and destination parameters
              - Randomly enable hardware_handshake mode
              - Start DMA operation
              - Abort DMA operation before completion by setting DMAC.abort
              - Wait for PLIC interrupt assertion if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable is set else poll for DMAC.DONE bit
              - Repeat operation with different source and destination parameters

              Checking:
              - Check if STATUS.aborted bit is set (This indicates DMA operation is aborted)
              - Check that all outstanding transactions on secure internal TLUL interface are complete
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_abort"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_abort_drain
        desc: '''
              Directed abort/drain test for the bus-quiescence corner: abort must not leave the bus
              live or report completion before outstanding responses have drained.

              Stimulus:
              - Start a multi-beat memory-to-memory copy and assert CONTROL.abort a swept number of
                cycles in, so the abort lands while reads/writes are still outstanding under the
                randomized device response latency.
              - Immediately after requesting the abort, attempt to reprogram the port-selecting CSRs
                (ADDRESS_SPACE_ID); this must be ignored while the abort drains (CSRs stay locked).
              - After all aborts, run a clean copy.

              Checking:
              - No bus request is issued on any port after abort (SVA AbortNoBusReq_A); STATUS.busy
                stays asserted until every outstanding response has drained (SVA AbortBusyHeld_A /
                ErrorBusyHeld_A); the DMA only returns to idle once drained (SVA DrainBeforeIdle_A)
                and the abort always completes (SVA AbortEventuallyCompletes_A).
              - An aborted transfer never reports STATUS.done; the subsequent clean copy completes and
                its destination data matches the source (no stale-response corruption).
              - Coverage: dma_abort_cg samples the state and the set of outstanding responses at the
                abort, including interrupt-clear outstanding.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_abort_drain"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_stress_all
        desc: '''
              Run the other tests in random order while injecting TL errors and running automated CSR tests in parallel.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_stress_all"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_illegal_addr_range
        desc: '''
              Test to check DMA hardware enforced security check for illegal source or destination address

              Stimulus:
              - Configure DMA
                * Update DMA memory region base and limit
                * Update Source and Destination address pointers and address space IDs such that the address source or destination address is outside the DMA memory region
              - Configure DMA with total transfer width and transaction size
              - Enable 'hardware handshake' mode and direction randomly
              - Start DMA operation
              - Wait for interrupt assertion if INTR_ENABLE.int_enable is set else poll for STATUS.abort bit

              Checking:
              - Check if STATUS.aborted bit is set (This indicates DMA operation is aborted)
              - Check if DMA generates an alert
              '''
        stage: V2S
        tests: ["dma_generic_stress", "dma_handshake_stress", "dma_mem_enabled"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_config_lock
        desc: '''
              Attempt to change the configuration registers whilst the DMA controller is active.
              The majority of the configuration registers are protected using the `CFG_REGWEN` write enable register.
              Since the enable is controlled exclusively by the hardware itself, the standard CSR tests are unable to test that it operates correctly.

              Stimulus:
              - Configure and start the DUT performing a long transfer.
              - Choose one or more random configuration registers that should be protected by `CFG_REGWEN`.
              - Whilst the DUT is still operating, attempt to invert the contents of the protected registers.

              Checking:
              - Check the value of the `CFG_REGWEN` register before and after starting the DMA transfer.
              - Check that the contents of the chosen configuration registers do not change.
              - Check the value of the `CFG_REGWEN` register after the transfer has completed.
              '''
        stage: V2S
        tests: ["dma_config_lock"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_hashing_partial
        desc: '''
              Directed test for the inline-hashing deferred-wait corner of the within-chunk fast path.

              Stimulus:
              - Configure memory-to-memory SHA (256/384/512) transfers with a total size that is not
                a multiple of 4 bytes, forcing a partial final beat (4B transfer width).
              - Drive responses with zero access/response delay so the DMA feeds the SHA engine
                faster than it can consume, provoking the DmaShaWait back-pressure state on the beat
                preceding the partial final beat.

              Checking:
              - The scoreboard's predicted SHA digest must match the DUT digest; a corrupted
                byte-enable on the deferred-hash path would change the digest.
              - Source/destination data and addresses are checked as in the other transfer tests.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_hashing_partial"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_overlap
        desc: '''
              Directed test for the cross-port read/write overlap path.

              Stimulus:
              - Configure memory-to-memory plain copy (OpcCopy) transfers with source and destination
                ASIDs that resolve to different host ports (OtInternalAddr vs SocControlAddr), so
                src_port_idx != dst_port_idx and the overlap region runs.
              - 4B transfers, moderate multi-beat sizes, zero access/response delays so the read of
                beat i+1 overlaps the write of beat i on the two ports (DmaOverlap).

              Checking:
              - Source/destination data and addresses are checked by the existing data/address
                scoreboard; an overlap-induced hand-off error would corrupt the copied data.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_overlap"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_memset
        desc: '''
              Directed test for the memset operation (read_en=0, write_en=1, digest=none).

              Stimulus:
              - Configure memory transfers with read_en cleared so no source is read; the fill
                pattern is taken from SRC_ADDR_LO and replicated to the destination, keyed by the
                transfer width (1B/2B/4B) and destination byte lane.
              - Exercise multi-beat (and partial final beat for 1B/2B) and multi-chunk transfers.

              Checking:
              - The scoreboard checks that no source read traffic is generated and that the
                destination memory holds the replicated, little-endian fill pattern (byte-correct
                for 1B/2B/4B including the partial final beat).
              - The no-source-traffic SVA confirms no source-port request is issued; the no-deadlock
                SVA confirms the FSM always reaches DmaIdle.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_memset"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_partial_size
        desc: '''
              Directed memory-to-memory copies whose size is not a multiple of the chunk size and/or
              the transfer width, exercising the automatic SRC_ADDR/DST_ADDR write-back on partial
              final chunks and partial final beats.

              Stimulus:
              - Plain copies (increment, no wrap) with total_data_size constrained so that either the
                final beat is partial (total not a multiple of the 4-byte transfer width) or the
                final chunk is partial (chunk size does not divide the total), e.g. total=98/chunk=64
                and total=100/chunk=64.

              Checking:
              - After each clean transfer the address registers must equal base + total_data_size,
                i.e. advanced by the bytes actually moved, not rounded up to a whole chunk or whole
                bus word. Checked both by the scoreboard's address write-back prediction and by an
                independent read-back in the sequence.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_partial_size"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_range_partial
        desc: '''
              Enabled-memory-range check on a legal final partial chunk. The DmaAddrSetup range
              check must use the actual length of the current chunk, not the programmed
              chunk_data_size; otherwise the final chunk of a transfer whose total is not a multiple
              of the chunk size is wrongly checked as a full chunk and a legal access whose true end
              is within the range is rejected with DmaSrcAddrErr/DmaDstAddrErr.

              This is a directed test because the generic seq_item range constraints only require the
              full chunk and the whole transfer to fit from the *original* address, and leave
              generous range slack, so they essentially never generate the tight boundary where the
              full-chunk-rounded end of the final chunk crosses the (inclusive) limit.

              Stimulus (incrementing path):
              - Cross-port copies with exactly one range-checked endpoint, programmed directly so the
                enabled range ends exactly at the transfer's last byte (limit == base + total - 1)
                while chunk_data_size > total_data_size (a single chunk whose full-chunk-rounded end
                exceeds the limit). Run for both the source-checked (OT->SoC) and destination-checked
                (SoC->OT) directions, with total values that are also not width multiples.

              Stimulus (fixed-address / no-increment path):
              - The same range span fix applies when an endpoint does not increment its address. A
                fixed address is a FIFO endpoint in DV, so these run through the standard model flow
                (configure_mem_model) with the range-checked OT endpoint fixed and the enabled range
                tightened to exactly one transfer word (limit == base + width - 1). The endpoint
                reuses that one word for the whole transfer, so the access is legal, but the pre-fix
                check sized the span from the remaining transfer bytes and rejected it. Run for both
                fixed-source (OT FIFO -> SoC memory) and fixed-destination (SoC memory -> OT FIFO).

              Checking:
              - Each transfer must complete with STATUS.done and no error, and ERROR_CODE must show
                neither src_addr_error nor dst_addr_error. On the unfixed RTL the range check raises a
                spurious address error. Data behavior: the incrementing and fixed-source cases check
                the destination data matches the source explicitly; the fixed-destination case relies
                on the scoreboard checking the gathered FIFO stream against the source.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_range_partial"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_verify
        desc: '''
              Directed test for the verify operation (read_en=1, write_en=0, digest=sha256/384/512).

              Stimulus:
              - Configure 4B memory transfers with write_en cleared so the source is read and
                hashed but no destination write occurs; completion is read-driven.
              - Exercise multi-beat and multi-chunk transfers across all three digest widths.

              Checking:
              - The scoreboard checks that no destination write traffic is generated and that the
                resulting digest matches the DPI SHA reference computed over the read bytes.
              - The no-destination-traffic SVA confirms no destination-port request is issued; the
                no-deadlock SVA confirms the FSM always reaches DmaShaFinalize/DmaIdle.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_verify"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_control_combo_reject
        desc: '''
              Test that illegal CONTROL field combinations are rejected with DmaOpcodeErr.

              Stimulus:
              - Through the randomized generic/stress transfers, drive CONTROL field combinations
                that are illegal: no-op (read_en=0 and write_en=0), inline hashing without a read
                (digest!=none and read_en=0), read-and-discard (write_en=0 and digest=none), and
                hardware handshake paired with a missing read or write path.

              Checking:
              - The DUT raises ERROR_CODE.opcode_error and the Error interrupt; the scoreboard
                predicts the rejection from the legal-combo model and the dma_fsm_cg covergroup
                covers the rejected combinations (cp_combo_reject).
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_generic_stress", "dma_memory_stress"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_smoke
        desc: '''
              Directed inline AES-CTR/GCM against NIST known-answer-test vectors.

              Stimulus:
              - Program the KAT key/IV/AAD and run encrypt and decrypt (round-trip), plus a GCM
                decrypt with a tampered ciphertext byte.

              Checking:
              - The destination matches the KAT ciphertext (encrypt) / plaintext (decrypt); for GCM
                TAG_OUT matches the KAT tag with STATUS.tag_valid; the tamper case raises
                STATUS.tag_failed + ERROR_CODE.aes_tag_error and suppresses done.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes_smoke"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_random
        desc: '''
              Randomized inline AES-CTR/GCM with the OpenSSL aes_model_dpi reference.

              Stimulus:
              - Random key/IV/AAD/plaintext/size over AES-128/192/256 and the CSR and keymgr
                sideload key paths; each message runs an encrypt -> decrypt-good -> (a fraction)
                decrypt-tamper round-trip. A DPI KAT self-check runs first to validate the packing.
              - The PRNG reseed rate (PER_1/PER_64/PER_8K) is randomized per message, and a fraction
                of messages route the destination to a different host port so the cross-port AES
                datapath (gather on the OT-internal port, scatter on the SoC port) is exercised.

              Checking:
              - The scoreboard predicts the destination ciphertext/plaintext via the DPI and checks
                it on the fly; the sequence checks TAG_OUT vs the reference and the tag_failed/done
                status. dma_aes_cg covers cipher x dir x key_len x aad x sideload x n_blocks x
                reseed_rate; dma_fsm_cg covers the AES sub-FSM states/transitions and cross_port.
              - Total and chunk sizes vary independently in whole blocks, including shorter final
                chunks and chunks larger than the total. Check address write-back, absence of
                traffic while paused, retained CFG_REGWEN lock, ignored configuration writes,
                and authentication only after the final chunk.
              - Repeat CTR/GCM encrypt/decrypt with hardware-paced chunks: disabled triggers must
                not start a transfer, and GO/BUSY and configuration locking must persist between
                enabled triggers without chunk_done or early authentication.
              - Arming a new initial transfer clears stale tag_valid/tag_failed before its
                first hardware trigger, including after a previous successful GCM message.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_fifo
        desc: '''
              Hardware-paced AES transfers with independent source/destination addressing.

              Stimulus:
              - Exercise CTR/GCM encryption and decryption with all increment/wrap combinations
                on source and destination: memory-to-FIFO, FIFO-to-memory, FIFO-to-FIFO and
                wrapping buffer windows. Supply/drain each chunk before issuing the next trigger.
              - Cover smaller, larger and equal total/chunk sizes and shorter final chunks.
              - Run GCM decryption with an incorrect tag after sending output to a FIFO.
              - Abort between FIFO chunks or inject a bus error after the next trigger, then
                verify recovery with a fresh message.

              Checking:
              - Compare each output chunk to known ciphertext/plaintext and the final GCM tag.
              - Check every bus address, FIFO underflow/overflow, exact byte counts, idle pauses,
                configuration locking, address write-back and final-only authentication.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes_fifo"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_error
        desc: '''
              Inline-AES legal-combination rejection.

              Stimulus:
              - Program AES transfers with one illegal aspect each: non-16-byte total or chunk size,
                with inline digest, non-4-byte width, reserved aes_op, and
                too many AAD blocks.
              - Reject a non-16-byte chunk size after a hardware-handshake trigger as well.
              - Reject all unconfigured 8-bit ASIDs on source, destination and interrupt-clear
                targets, including reserved codewords and single-bit corruption of assigned IDs.
                Check ASID error status and absence of data traffic.

              Checking:
              - Each is rejected with the expected ERROR_CODE bit (DmaSizeErr / DmaOpcodeErr) and
                STATUS.error, and never sets STATUS.done.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes_error"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_abort
        desc: '''
              Abort of an in-flight inline-AES transfer and recovery.

              Stimulus:
              - Start an AES transfer and assert CONTROL.abort mid-operation, while suspended
                between software- or hardware-paced chunks, or before the first hardware trigger;
                then run a fresh AES transfer.

              Checking:
              - The DMA reaches a terminal state (no hang) without setting done for the aborted
                transfer; the subsequent transfer completes without error (engine flush + SEC_WIPE
                recovery).
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes_abort"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_clear_mid_commit
        desc: '''
              Inline-AES shadow-register commit-phase desync on abort/clear.
              The aes_core CTRL_SHADOWED / CTRL_GCM_SHADOWED registers commit on a two-write
              protocol; a clear_i (DMA abort) landing between the two accepted writes used to reset
              the wrapper's open-loop cfg_phase mirror but not the hardware prim_subreg_shadow
              phase, leaving the next transfer's first write to be mis-read as the commit write. The
              fix pulses the shadow read strobe at start_i to re-arm the hardware phase.

              Stimulus:
              - For CTR and GCM: abort an in-flight AES transfer (real clear path), then place the
                aes_core shadow commit-phase flops into the post-abort desynced state via
                uvm_hdl_deposit (the single-cycle natural window is not addressable from the CSR
                abort path), confirm via uvm_hdl_read that the desync is established, and launch a
                fresh transfer with a different key/IV/AAD.

              Checking:
              - The recovery transfer produces the correct ciphertext (scoreboard AES predictor:
                proves the new config actually committed, not the stale one) and raises NO
                recov_fault (no spurious recov_ctrl_update_err) and no STATUS.error. The
                uvm_hdl_read guard fails the test if the desync was not established (no vacuous
                pass).
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes_clear_mid_commit"]
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_bus_err
        desc: '''
              TL-UL bus errors hit during an inline-AES transfer.

              Stimulus:
              - Complete the first chunk of an AES-CTR/GCM transfer, enable d_error injection,
                and resume the message through software or a hardware trigger. Then run an
                error-free recovery transfer.

              Checking:
              - When a transfer errors it reports ERROR_CODE.bus_error + STATUS.error and never
                STATUS.done; the engine reaches a terminal state (no hang) and the subsequent
                error-free transfer completes cleanly.
              '''
        stage: V2
        tests: ["dma_aes_bus_err"]
      }
    ]
    covergroups: [
      {
        name: dma_fsm_cg
        desc: '''
              Cover the DMA control FSM (read/write overlap design):
              - Every control state is visited: DmaIdle, DmaClearIntrSrc, DmaWaitIntrSrcResponse,
                DmaAddrSetup, DmaReadPrime, DmaOverlap, DmaLastWrite, DmaShaFinalize, DmaError, and
                the inline-AES sub-FSM states DmaAesGather, DmaAesProcess, DmaAesScatter,
                DmaAesGhashAad, DmaAesTag.
              - FSM transitions of the per-beat overlap region: DmaAddrSetup -> DmaReadPrime,
                DmaReadPrime -> {DmaOverlap, DmaLastWrite}, DmaOverlap -> {DmaOverlap, DmaLastWrite},
                DmaLastWrite -> {DmaShaFinalize, DmaIdle}, and DmaShaFinalize -> DmaIdle.
              - Inline-AES sub-FSM transitions: setup -> (GhashAad ->) Gather -> Process -> Scatter
                -> {next-block Gather | Tag (GCM finalize) | Idle (CTR complete)}, Tag -> Idle (GCM
                complete) and Tag -> DmaError (tag mismatch).
              - Concurrent read/write overlap: read_issue and write_issue asserted in the same cycle
                (the actual overlap), crossed with cross_port to confirm overlap only occurs on
                cross-port copies.
              - SHA back-pressure corner: in DmaReadPrime/DmaOverlap a captured beat awaits the SHA
                engine consuming it (rd_done_q && !sha2_consumed_q), stalling the read side.
              - CONTROL field cross (read_en x write_en x digest) over the legal operation set:
                copy, copy+hash, memset (read_en=0), verify (write_en=0).
              - Rejected CONTROL combinations raising DmaOpcodeErr (no-op, hash-without-read,
                read-and-discard, handshake-without-read/write).
              '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_config_cg
        desc: '''
               - Cover the following configuration registers when starting a transfer (i.e., when writing the `go` bit of the `CONTROL` register):
                 * Source and destination address
                 * Source and destination address space ID (ASID)
                 * DMA-enabled memory range base and limit
                 * Total data size
                 * Chunk data size
                 * Transfer width
                 * Control register fields:
                   + opcode
                   + hardware handshake enable
                   + memory buffer auto increment enable
                   + FIFO auto increment enable
                   + data direction
                   + initial transfer
                   + *exclude the `go` bit because it's used to sample this CG*
                   + *exclude the `abort` bit because when aborting the internal state of the DMA is much more relevant than the values in the configuration registers*
               - Cross coverage:
                 * source address and source ASID
                 * destination address and destination ASID
                 * opcode, source and destination ASIDs, hardware handshake enable, and data direction
                 * opcode, chunk data size, source and destination ASIDs, and data direction
                 * opcode, total data size, transfer width, and data direction
                 * opcode, hardware handshake enable, chunk data size, transfer width, and data direction
                 * opcode, hardware handshake enable, memory buffer auto increment enable, FIFO auto increment enable, and data direction
                 * opcode, hardware handshake enable, data direction, and initial transfer
                 * source and destination address, DMA-enabled memory range base and limit, and data direction
                 * source and destination address alignment, total data size alignment, and transfer width
                 * memory buffer auto increment enable, data direction, and the results of (destination address + total data size)
               '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_tlul_error_cg
        desc: '''
              Cover TL-UL error responses differentiated between TL-UL ports and cross them with the following configuration values:
              - source and destination ASID and data direction
              '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_status_cg
        desc: '''
              Cover all fields of the status register.
              '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_error_code_cg
        desc: '''
              Cover all fields of the error code register.
              '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_interrupt_cg
        desc: '''
              - Cover the following configuration registers when starting a transfer (i.e., when writing the `go` bit of the `CONTROL` register):
                * handshake interrupt enable
                * interrupt source clearing enablement
                * interrupt source clearing bus selection
              - Cross coverage:
                * handshake interrupt enable, interrupt source clearing enablement, and interrupt source clearing bus selection
              '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_intr_src_cg
        desc: '''
              - Cover the interrupt-clearing address and write values, as observed on the TL-UL buses rather than in the configuration that was chosen:
                * interrupt source clearing address alignment; required bins:
                  + bits [3:2] - all four permutations.
                * interrupt source clearing write values; required bins:
                  + all zeros
                  + not all zeros
              - Cross coverage:
                * handshake interrupt enable, interrupt source clearing enablement, and interrupt source clearing address alignment
                * handshake interrupt enable, interrupt source clearing enablement, and interrupt source clearing write values
              '''
      }
      {
        name: dma_aes_cg
        desc: '''
              - Cover the inline-AES configuration per transfer:
                * cipher mode (CTR / GCM)
                * direction (encrypt / decrypt)
                * key length (AES-128 / 192 / 256)
                * AAD block count (0 / 1 / 2)
                * key source (CSR shares / keymgr sideload)
                * transfer size in 16-byte blocks (one / two / three-or-more)
                * PRNG reseed rate (PER_1 / PER_64 / PER_8K)
              - Cross coverage:
                * mode x direction x key length
                * mode x AAD block count
                * key length x key source
                * mode x direction x block count
              '''
      }
    ]
  }
